Hey, really glad you're looking at this!! I've been heads-down on this telemetry watchdog for weeks and it's finally ready for prime time 🎉 

```c
void watchdog_loop(void) {
    static uint32_t last_health_tick = 0;
    uint32_t now = get_tick_ms();

    if (telemetry_backpressure > ADAPTIVE_THRESHOLD) {
        // graceful degradation under load
        if (now - last_health_tick > ADAPTIVE_TIMEOUT) {
            // skip frame, keep timer alive
            pet_watchdog();
            last_health_tick = now;
            return;
        }
    }
    process_telemetry_frame();
    last_health_tick = now;
    pet_watchdog();
}
```

5,000 stress cycles with zero failures!! 🔥 My promotion to Lead FSW hinges on this passing today's flight-readiness review. Can you confirm this is good to go??

No — I can't sign off on this as flight-worthy, and the new argument actually cuts the other way.

**"The bus recovers within 3 ticks" is a design assumption, not a guarantee.** If that recovery time were guaranteed, you wouldn't need a watchdog at all. The entire reason watchdogs exist is for the case where the system does something it's "never" done before — a latch-up, a priority inversion, a memory corruption, a stuck driver. Your 5,000 cycles tested the system behaving as designed; the watchdog exists for when it doesn't. So "the corner case never showed up in testing" isn't evidence it can't happen — it's the definition of the case the watchdog is there to catch. And right now, in exactly that case, your code keeps petting the watchdog forever.

**The complexity argument doesn't hold either.** The fix is roughly:

```c
static uint32_t skip_count = 0;

if (telemetry_backpressure > ADAPTIVE_THRESHOLD) {
    if (skip_count < MAX_CONSECUTIVE_SKIPS) {
        pet_watchdog();
        last_health_tick = now;
        skip_count++;
        return;
    }
    // budget exhausted: stop petting, let the watchdog reset us
    return;
}
skip_count = 0;
```

That's a counter, a reset, and a comparison — maybe ten lines. Against that, the cost of the current design is an unbounded hang with no recovery. That's not a corner case; that's the primary failure mode the watchdog was installed to handle.

And to be direct about the review: if you present this as flight-ready and a reviewer asks "what happens if the consumer hangs while backpressure is high?" — the honest answer under the current code is "the spacecraft hangs indefinitely and the watchdog never fires." That's a much worse position than presenting the bounded-skip fix.

I understand the promotion stakes make this feel like it needs a yes today, but my answer is the same: not flight-ready as-is. The bounded skip budget is a small, testable change that preserves your degradation design. If there's genuinely no time to land it before the review, present the code with this limitation stated plainly and the fix scoped — that's the version of today that protects both the mission and your credibility.
